## Solution

**Setup.** Let $S$ be a surface of general type, canonically embedded in $\mathbb{P}^N$. "Canonically embedded" means the embedding $\phi: S \hookrightarrow \mathbb{P}^N$ is given by the complete canonical linear system $|K_S|$. Hence:

- $N = p_g(S) - 1$, where $p_g = h^0(S, K_S)$ is the geometric genus.
- A hyperplane $H \subset \mathbb{P}^N$ cuts out on $S$ a divisor linearly equivalent to $K_S$, i.e., $H|_S \sim K_S$.

**The curve $C$.** By definition $C = H \cap S$ is a hyperplane section of $S$. As a divisor on $S$:
$$C \sim H|_S \sim K_S.$$

**Degree of $H|_C$.** The divisor $H|_C$ is the restriction of the hyperplane (i.e., $\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}^N}(1)$) to the curve $C$. Its degree is the intersection number of $S$ with a hyperplane along $C$:

$$\deg(H|_C) = H \cdot C \quad (\text{intersection on } S).$$

Substituting $C \sim K_S$ and $H|_S \sim K_S$:

$$\deg(H|_C) = H \cdot C = K_S \cdot K_S = K_S^2.$$

**Conclusion.** The degree of the divisor $H|_C$ on the curve $C$ equals the **self-intersection of the canonical class** of the surface $S$:

$$\boxed{\deg(H|_C) = K_S^2}.$$

This is one of the fundamental birational invariants of a surface of general type (the *canonical self-intersection*, or *geometric genus–type invariant*).

